00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.840
(URANIUM FROM THE COLONIES)
 &nbsp;

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:09.420
Fifty years ago, the famous oil crisis occurred.&nbsp;
The price of dinosaur juice multiplied by four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.320
after the Arab countries decided to&nbsp;
reduce their production. At that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.300
most of the electricity in France, a country&nbsp;
with no oil fields of its own, was generated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:24.060
oil-fired power plants compared to 5% provided&nbsp;
by the first generation of nuclear reactors.
 &nbsp;

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:29.580
VisualPolitik viewers, this crisis was key to&nbsp;
the activation of the Messmer plan. In 1974,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.400
Pierre Messmer, President&nbsp;
Pompidou's prime minister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.500
presented the plan by which France would end&nbsp;
its total energy dependence on foreign oil.
 &nbsp;

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.540
His goal was nothing less than to build dozens&nbsp;
of nuclear reactors. All it needed was uranium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.460
for its nuclear fuel. And the French Atomic&nbsp;
Energy Commission already knew where to find it:&nbsp;

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:53.700
Somair and Cominak have for decades been two of&nbsp;
the largest uranium mines in the world. France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:59.580
discovered uranium in Niger in 1957. So when this&nbsp;
African country gained its independence in 1960,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:05.880
Paris ensured that it maintained priority access&nbsp;
to Niger's natural resources by means of a treaty.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.280
And so, the large-scale exploitation of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:13.020
uranium mines started between the end&nbsp;
of the 1960s and the 1970s. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.580
some French leaders were able to deploy all the&nbsp;
bad practices they had already used in Gabon.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:27.660
You see, France began operating the Mounana&nbsp;
uranium mine in Gabon in 1960. In the late 1960s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.200
measurements recorded high levels&nbsp;
of gamma and alpha radiation among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.560
mine workers. Adopting the radiation&nbsp;
protection protocols used in France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.680
required costly investments and&nbsp;
the French were not in the mood.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.140
The solution adopted was much more cost-effective:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:44.700
tripling the thresholds above which radiation&nbsp;
exposure was considered dangerous to human health.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:52.200
This is one of the advantages that the mines&nbsp;
in the former colonies had for France. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:56.520
costs were much lower because neither the&nbsp;
safety nor the wages of the miners were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.900
same as in other countries. And at this point&nbsp;
I am sure that someone will be thinking: well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:05.400
if uranium mining has polluted a lot, at least&nbsp;
it will have left Niger with an economic benefit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.200
Well, no. The exploitation agreements&nbsp;
were abusively advantageous for France.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:15.300
For many decades, French mining companies&nbsp;
paid only 5.5% of their revenues in taxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:21.000
to Niger. In Canada and Kazakhstan, where Orano&nbsp;
has been producing uranium since the late 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:27.780
royalty rates are 13% and 18.5% respectively.&nbsp;
This has led to disparities as you can see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:32.460
this graph between the amount of uranium produced&nbsp;
and the revenue each country receives in return.&nbsp;

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.260
In 2014, a new agreement was&nbsp;
negotiated between Niger and Orano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:42.420
then called Areva. The royalty rate was&nbsp;
raised from 5.5% to a figure around 12%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:47.100
provided certain conditions of&nbsp;
profitability and uranium price were met.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.280
Conditions have improved slightly,&nbsp;
but France has continued to operate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.520
in Niger with a certain advantage&nbsp;
over the international market.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.360
If we add to this the fact that Niger is&nbsp;
one of the poorest countries in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.100
we can surely speak of yet another case of&nbsp;
the resource curse. This is what economists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:06.480
call it when they refer to a State that does&nbsp;
not benefit financially from the sale of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.200
resources. Even so, there is no greater&nbsp;
curse than the corruption of its leaders.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:20.460
In 1974, Lieutenant Colonel Seyni Kountché&nbsp;
staged a coup d'état. President until 1987,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:25.080
Kountché claimed to receive directly part&nbsp;
of the production of uranium oxide in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:29.700
percentage equal to the participation of the&nbsp;
State of Niger in the Nigerien subsidiaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.240
of the French mining companies.&nbsp;
A percentage of more than 30%.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:38.280
In this way, Kountché wanted to sell&nbsp;
the uranium on his own account on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.420
international market. The atomic intentions&nbsp;
of his customers were not exactly peaceful:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:47.820
Saddam Hussein's Iraq, Gaddafi's Libya,&nbsp;
Pakistan... Kountché is credited with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:51.300
the phrase: "We will sell uranium&nbsp;
even to the devil if necessary".

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.840
Now I know what you are thinking. Since&nbsp;
the military junta that has seized power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.340
in Niger is out for revenge against France,&nbsp;
they will surely break the agreements for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.900
exploitation of their mines, expel Orano&nbsp;
from the country and France will lose all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:09.060
the uranium in Niger. And of course, considering&nbsp;
that nuclear power plants generate about 70% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:13.620
France's electricity, if Niger's military&nbsp;
suddenly cut off the supply of uranium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:19.560
France's energy production would surely tremble.&nbsp;
Right? Well, no. Unlike Germany with Russian gas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.980
this time France has done its&nbsp;
homework and done it very well.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.320
France has been able to diversify its uranium&nbsp;
supply. According to data from the last 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:37.920
the 88,200 tons of natural uranium imported&nbsp;
by France came mainly from three countries:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:43.680
Kazakhstan (27%), Niger (20%) and&nbsp;
Uzbekistan (19%). Imports from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.660
Australia and Namibia also stand out, with&nbsp;
14% in each case. In terms of annual data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:54.300
uranium from Niger never accounted for more than&nbsp;
35% of French imports in a single fiscal year.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:02.760
These data are similar to those of Orano. In 2010,&nbsp;
Niger's mines provided two-thirds of its global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:07.980
production. However, Niger now provides less&nbsp;
than one-third after the Cominak mine closed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:13.560
in 2021. All this means that Niger's uranium,&nbsp;
once decisive, is now of relative importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:17.820
to the French energy system, accounting&nbsp;
for barely 20% of the uranium they import.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:22.620
Compare that to the 60% that Russian gas&nbsp;
meant for Germany or even the 90% that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:26.700
it meant for Eastern European countries like&nbsp;
the Czech Republic. In other words, if Niger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:31.140
cuts off the uranium tap, France could easily&nbsp;
replace it with imports from other countries.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:36.000
Now, that is not to say that the disruption&nbsp;
would have no effect. It would be a setback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.200
for its strategy of being an alternative&nbsp;
to Russia in the nuclear fuel market. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:49.860
am I talking about? Stay tuned.
 
(A MARKET DOMINATED BY RUSSIA)

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.480
Just as combined cycle power plants use&nbsp;
natural gas as fuel to generate electricity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:02.520
nuclear power plants need their own fuel. The&nbsp;
process most commonly used in atomic power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:07.260
plants to generate energy is nuclear fission.&nbsp;
For this they need an element that is fissile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:12.780
or easily split. This is where uranium comes into&nbsp;
play. Now, just as you have to refine petroleum to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:18.600
obtain fuels such as gasoline or diesel, you have&nbsp;
to enrich uranium to be able to use it as fuel in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:23.700
nuclear power plants. The process is the same as&nbsp;
the one used to develop atomic bombs. What changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:31.620
is the level of enrichment needed. Take a look.
Natural uranium, as extracted from the mines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:37.980
is composed of 99.3% non-fissile&nbsp;
uranium 238 isotopes. The fissile part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:43.740
uranium-235 isotopes, accounts for only&nbsp;
0.7%. Enriched to between 3% and 5%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:49.020
this uranium serves as nuclear fuel for power&nbsp;
plants that generate electricity. Up to 20%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:54.240
enriched uranium is used to produce medical&nbsp;
isotopes, which are used in particular in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:59.280
diagnosis of certain cancers. Finally, to make&nbsp;
a bomb, enrichment must be brought up to 90%.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:07.080
And guess what, right here is the key that has&nbsp;
led to the international nuclear fuel market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:11.460
being dominated by Russia. Traditionally,&nbsp;
uranium has been enriched by two methods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:15.060
The West opted for the simpler one:&nbsp;
gaseous diffusion. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:19.320
this simpler method was very expensive,&nbsp;
since it requires a lot of energy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:22.920
enrich the uranium. How much energy are we&nbsp;
talking about? Well, to give you an idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:28.920
the enrichment plant that France opened at the end&nbsp;
of the 1970s consumed 15 terawatt hours per year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:34.140
the same amount of electricity that would be used&nbsp;
today for public lighting in Paris for a century.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.080
During the three decades it was in operation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.000
the Georges Bessee uranium enrichment plant&nbsp;
was France's largest consumer of electricity;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.620
it devoured about 4% of France's entire&nbsp;
electricity production. In fact, at the height&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:55.680
of the Cold War, about 7% of total US electricity&nbsp;
generation was devoted to uranium enrichment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:04.740
for both military and civilian purposes.
Gaseous diffusion was the method used by the West.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:07.680
Now, what were they doing on the&nbsp;
other side of the iron curtain?

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:13.680
Well, the Soviet nuclear program is known to all.&nbsp;
Both in terms of armament and atomic energy. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:19.680
USSR started up some thirty reactors. As for&nbsp;
uranium enrichment, the Soviet Union opted for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:24.960
more complex technique: centrifugation. However,&nbsp;
this method consumes 50 times less energy than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:30.120
gaseous diffusion. This explains how today, in the&nbsp;
middle of 2023, we find news stories like this:

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:34.980
(Putin Still Selling U.S. Nearly $1&nbsp;
Billion in Nuclear Fuel – Newsweek)

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:39.600
When the Soviet Union fell in 1991,&nbsp;
the United States and the new Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:43.920
agreed to what is popularly known as&nbsp;
the "megaton-to-megawatt program."

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:53.460
Highly enriched uranium from former Soviet nuclear&nbsp;
warheads was converted into low-enriched uranium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.900
and shipped to the United States for use&nbsp;
in civilian plants. Also, given the cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:02.700
differential, Western electric utilities&nbsp;
rushed to acquire Russian enriched uranium.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:07.440
In doing so they benefited from the more&nbsp;
cost-competitive technology of the Soviet era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.040
the problem is that at the same&nbsp;
time they contributed to the ruin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.820
of those facilities in the West that&nbsp;
were still using gaseous diffusion.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:19.200
But of course, in those happy 1990s, the&nbsp;
only thing in danger of running out under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:23.820
Boris Yeltsin was our alcohol reserves.&nbsp;
So Russia achieved a position of market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:27.720
dominance that has greatly benefited&nbsp;
Rosatom, the State corporation created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:33.360
in 2007 by Vladimir Putin to be the Russian&nbsp;
giant in the global nuclear energy market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:37.920
A giant that is absolutely untouchable&nbsp;
even under the most adverse conditions.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:43.260
(Pressure mounts on EU to sanction Rosatom&nbsp;
in next Russia sanctions package – Euroactiv)

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:47.580
Neither Brussels nor Washington has&nbsp;
dared to sanction Rosatom's nuclear fuel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:51.060
The United States stopped enriching&nbsp;
uranium years ago and about a third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:55.260
of the nuclear fuel it currently uses is&nbsp;
imported from Russia, the world's largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:59.820
and also the cheapest producer. And yet the&nbsp;
bill rises each year to nearly $1 billion.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:04.800
So, in the midst of the war in Ukraine,&nbsp;
with sanctions on oil, on natural gas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:09.600
on thousands of Russian companies and products,&nbsp;
it would be ideal not to depend on the Kremlin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:13.140
for nuclear fuel. Right? Well, this is&nbsp;
where France's strategy comes into play.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:19.140
(THE NUCLEAR ÉPOQUE)

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:26.400
VisualPolitik community, if you are&nbsp;
watching this video from Germany or Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:32.400
you may be surprised. But nuclear is all the rage.&nbsp;
Currently, 59 nuclear reactors are being built in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:37.680
the world. These numbers are scheduled to increase&nbsp;
by another 111 in the next few years. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.900
there are plans for another 321. Forty&nbsp;
percent of these reactors are or will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:47.640
be located in China. But other powers such as&nbsp;
Russia and India are also investing heavily in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:52.080
nuclear power. Even in the United States there&nbsp;
are serious plans to relaunch this industry.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.320
And hold on a moment, because in Europe&nbsp;
there are countries that do not want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.740
be left behind. France already announced&nbsp;
before the war in Ukraine that it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:02.760
build six new reactors. But besides&nbsp;
those, the United Kingdom, Poland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:07.620
the Netherlands, Slovenia, Slovakia, the&nbsp;
Czech Republic, Hungary, Bulgaria, Romania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:13.080
Estonia and Lithuania also have nuclear power&nbsp;
among their plans. And it is possible that more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:17.160
countries will join after the backing given&nbsp;
by the European Union to this technology.

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:22.500
(EU parliament backs labelling gas and&nbsp;
nuclear investments as green – Reuters)

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:25.800
The energy transition and the fight&nbsp;
against climate change will require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:29.940
investment in new technologies. Also in&nbsp;
everything that involves maintaining our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:33.420
quality of life while reducing&nbsp;
greenhouse gas emissions. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:38.640
in this area that Brussels has decided that&nbsp;
renewables and nuclear must go hand in hand.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:42.480
What’s more, the new modular reactors&nbsp;
are completely changing the reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:46.440
of this industry: they require much less&nbsp;
investment and can be built much faster.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:51.480
For these reasons, European countries are ready&nbsp;
to give atomic energy another chance. They plan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:56.220
build new power plants and, of course, the most&nbsp;
important thing will be needed: nuclear fuel.

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:02.700
It will be necessary to increase&nbsp;
uranium production in the mines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:06.720
yes, but also to increase the&nbsp;
capacity to process that uranium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:10.920
convert it and enrich it. And this is&nbsp;
exactly where France wants to play a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:15.420
leading role. It is already working on it,&nbsp;
as reflected in news headlines such as this:

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:20.220
(France Mulls New Uranium Plant to Cut&nbsp;
West's Reliance on Russia – Bloomberg)

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:25.200
According to an Orano spokesman, the French&nbsp;
state-owned company is considering two options: on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:30.300
the one hand, building a uranium enrichment plant&nbsp;
in the United States; on the other, an option that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:35.940
would be the quickest and most likely: expanding&nbsp;
by 50% the capacity of its modern Georges Besse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:41.220
Two plant, operational since 2011 and already&nbsp;
enriching uranium using the centrifuge technique.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:47.820
Enriched uranium is measured in SWU, separative&nbsp;
work units. At its Georges Besse Two plant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:54.000
France enriches 7.5 million SWU per year.&nbsp;
Increasing it by 50% would raise its capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:59.280
to 11 million, whereas French nuclear power&nbsp;
plants currently need about 6.5 million SWU.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:04.440
This corresponds to the approximately 8,000&nbsp;
tons of natural uranium that France needs for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:09.900
its nuclear power plants. If it plans to produce&nbsp;
11 million SWU, it will need to import more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:15.120
13,000 tons of uranium each year. And this is&nbsp;
where Niger could create a problem for France.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:23.580
As I was telling you earlier, its own energy&nbsp;
system can afford to do without the nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:27.300
2,000 tons it imports every year from&nbsp;
Niger. It would find other suppliers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:31.740
But France does need this uranium for its&nbsp;
strategy of becoming a major exporter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:35.460
nuclear fuel and a real alternative&nbsp;
to Russian domination. In any case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:40.260
there is no doubt that Macron is working in this&nbsp;
direction. And that explains news items like this:

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:43.860
(Uranium from Mongolia interests France - LeMonde)

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:48.180
Macron and his foreign minister have&nbsp;
visited Mongolia in recent months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:52.200
There they have shown interest in making&nbsp;
investments worth about one billion euros&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:57.300
to get hold of Mongolia's uranium as well as&nbsp;
other metals such as lithium. Of course, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.300 --> 00:18:01.860
Ulaanbaatar they have jumped at the opportunity&nbsp;
and have welcomed Macron with open arms. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:06.240
even say that France is the third neighbor&nbsp;
of Mongolia, that giant country wedged like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:11.640
a sandwich between Russia and China. Along these&nbsp;
lines, a major agreement is expected very soon.

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:16.860
Of course, this does not detract from the fact&nbsp;
that, for the moment, France has a major headache&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:22.440
with Niger. Don't forget that the power that is&nbsp;
becoming strong in the Sahel is Russia, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:27.240
could suddenly find itself in Niger with a new&nbsp;
uranium supplier. Something that would undoubtedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:31.500
contribute to further consolidate its position&nbsp;
of dominance over the nuclear fuel market.

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:34.320
And this means that if the world&nbsp;
continues to bet on atomic energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:38.340
we will all, including the West,&nbsp;
increase our dependence on Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:42.780
thus increasing the power of Putin and the&nbsp;
Kremlin. That at least seems to be Moscow's idea.

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.520
But, now the questions are over to you:

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:48.900
Do you think France will succeed in its strategy,&nbsp;
or will Russia always win in the energy game? You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:52.680
can leave me your answer in the comments. In the&nbsp;
meantime, don't forget that we release new videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:57.000
every week so subscribe to VisualPolitik&nbsp;
so you don't miss any of our updates. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:01.560
you liked this video, LIKE it and see you in&nbsp;
the next one. All the best and see you soon.

